WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.338
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.574 --> 00:00:07.240
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.060
- Did you know Android has built
in anti-virus protection that

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.100
gets more powerful every second?

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.440
It starts when an Android
developer uploads their app

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.810
to Google Play.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.030
A self-learning at-risk
analyzer scans and simulates

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.760
apps, looking for
potential security issues.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.210
If there's an issue, the app
won't appear in Google Play.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.970
On Android devices,
Verify Apps regularly

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.420
scans for harmful behavior,
warning users, and then sending

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.140
this information
to the analyzer,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.780
so it keeps getting smarter.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.870
SafetyNet also scans devices
for exploits and network

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.680
vulnerabilities, protecting more
than one billion devices daily

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.870
and, again, sending data
back to the analyzer

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.850
to get more powerful.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.410
Android and Google Play--

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.830
constantly making your
mobile device safer at home,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.194
work, and everywhere in between.

00:00:52.194 --> 00:00:56.146
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.860
EDWARD CUNNINGHAM:
Well, good afternoon.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.090
I hope the video has given you a
clue as to which session you're

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:08.320
in.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.530
So this talk is going to
be about Google security

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.150
services for Android.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:16.180
My name is Edward Cunningham,
and I'm a project manager

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.930
on the Android security team.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.070
So I hope that some of you may
have caught the talk yesterday

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.717
by Adrian Ludwig, who leads
Android security, talking

00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:29.300
about the overview
of Android security

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.090
and its position
in the ecosystem.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.770
If you didn't catch
that, I recommend

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.470
going and checking out the
video that will be online.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.630
But I'll begin with a bit
of an introduction that

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:44.900
helps frame Google's services
for Android security fit-in.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.250
And there'll be lots of time
at the end for questions,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.966
so I hope we can have
a good discussion.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.920
Android has become a part of
the fabric of our daily lives,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.870
and nearly all of us
interact with Android

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.540
in some form each
day, whether that's

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.730
through the phone you use--

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.610
85% of smartphones
are Android phones--

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.090
or, perhaps, the phone of
someone you communicate with,

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.030
or maybe the TV you have--
that could be running Android--

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:20.560
or the point of sale system that
you use in a store you visit.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.340
And securing all of these
devices and different use cases

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.550
is what many of us at Google
on the Android security team

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.010
get excited by each
day as our mission.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.200
But it's not just Google's job.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.930
Insuring and driving the
security of this ecosystem

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:42.520
is something that is performed
by our partners, OEMs,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:48.190
enterprises, and developers
too, so improving

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.920
the security of
this vast ecosystem

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.727
is an exciting challenge.

00:02:52.727 --> 00:02:55.060
In this talk, I'm going to
give you an insight into some

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.080
of Google's many services
for Android security

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.986
and give specific
examples of ways

00:03:01.986 --> 00:03:03.985
that you could take
advantage of these services.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.360
The Android ecosystem
consists of many millions

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.140
of devices and apps and
use cases, but at its core,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.750
consists of the
people who use them.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.110
Android likely has
the most diverse set

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:26.067
of users of any
computing platform today.

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:27.900
And the range of threats
that they encounter

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.025
drives their security models
and the protections we

00:03:30.025 --> 00:03:32.860
put in place.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.190
At one end of the spectrum,
we have those who face,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.150
perhaps, the most advanced
adversaries, the likes

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.360
of world leaders, who are
investing hundreds of thousands

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.360
of dollars to protect
their communications.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.310
And last year, we had
that President Obama

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.700
had started using Android
phone for his presidential

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:51.510
communications.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.231
And his successor appears
to have done likewise.

00:03:55.231 --> 00:03:56.980
But of course, Android
security can't just

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:02.510
be about those who can afford
to pay a premium for security.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.430
Android phones are
a critical lifeline

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:09.080
for many of the most oppressed
groups and persecuted people

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.610
in the world today,
such as refugees,

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:16.240
who could be fleeing
from oppressive regimes.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.940
Android brings them a way
to communicate, to navigate,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.490
and it includes powerful
security protections

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.700
for those who can't afford
to pay an extra dollar, euro,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:32.390
or pound for that security.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:36.140
Another example where the
security guarantees in Android

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:40.890
are driving new
businesses and use cases

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.806
is in the realm of gaming.

00:04:42.806 --> 00:04:44.180
Scenes like you
see in the middle

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.260
here were common last summer
when the Pokemon Go phenomenon

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.610
kicked off.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.480
The core of Pokemon
Go's business model

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.020
is a sophisticated approach
to detecting abuse,

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:02.530
and many other games
rely on this similarly.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.890
Specifically, Pokemon Go
uses Google's APIs on Android

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.010
to detect fraudulent
uses and devices,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.490
who would be performing
fraud in their game.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.320
And you'll have seen
at the talk yesterday

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.650
that all Android devices
have a common set of security

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:22.790
properties.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.750
This is what we describe
as the platform security.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:27.710
This is built into the
Android open source

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.790
project, the foundational
layers of security.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:35.120
This includes things like
application isolation.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.800
The key part of this
is the sandboxes

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.560
that protect apps, make
sure the data inside apps

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.370
is for the app to use only,
and that another app can't

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.500
peer into the data
from any other app.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.390
Device integrity is important.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.820
Features like
verified boot ensure

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.340
the integrity of
the system partition

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.110
with cryptographic checks.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.180
And data encryption,
full disk encryption,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:00.300
makes sure the data,
user data, is protected.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.440
Android has advanced
exploit mitigation features,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.970
things like address space
layout randomization or integer

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.220
overflow protection
ensure that it's

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.160
hard to exploit vulnerabilities
in the platform,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.290
even if they are present.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.090
And lastly, management
capabilities--

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.600
Android has built
powerful capabilities,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.630
such as work profiles
or administrative APIs

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.820
to allow you to manage devices.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.130
This is obviously
important in being

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:32.730
able to deploy Android
devices in many different use

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:36.200
cases, such as your enterprise.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.010
So across all modern
Android phones,

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.890
there's a relatively
uniform set of capabilities.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:45.580
In fact, it could be said
that across Android and iOS

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.340
and Chrome OS, all
modern mobile operating

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.920
systems, that set of
capabilities is pretty similar.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.140
And this is how the
future of cloud computing

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.100
is being shaped.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:03.120
Android is-- one of the key
parts of Android, I guess,

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.070
is the research
community around it.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.650
Being an open source
program, we have

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.200
hundreds of researchers,
developers around the world

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.510
contributing to that security.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.790
Last year, Android's
vulnerability rewards program

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.750
paid out over a million
dollars to researchers.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.260
And as we build this ecosystem
of research and development

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.860
around Android security,
we're bringing Android

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.940
to new heights of
security as we grow

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:33.650
this protection year on year
through the platform releases.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.200
On top of this solid
foundation, Google

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.990
builds security services
to protect users

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.550
from the risks they might face.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:45.320
Building services on top of
platforms is at Google's core.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.400
Like Google wasn't originally
a platform provider,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.770
and so this is something
that's being built into Android

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.450
from the very first minute.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.180
And this is where
SafetyNet comes in.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.340
SafetyNet is the
collection of services

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.010
that Google builds
and freely deploys

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:07.036
to protect users and
keep the ecosystem safe.

00:08:07.036 --> 00:08:08.660
Our mission is to
protect Android users

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.347
with data-driven security
apps and services.

00:08:11.347 --> 00:08:13.680
And this is what we're going
to look at in this session.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.230
We provide comprehensive
endpoint protection

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.660
out of the box.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.580
From the very first moment a
user starts using their device,

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.310
they should have all the
necessary protections in place

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:31.495
to be and feel safe.

00:08:31.495 --> 00:08:35.030
The out of the box part
is very important to us.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.460
We don't think a
user should ever

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.900
need to go and invest
more money in security

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.150
once they've got their device.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.730
It's a fundamental
part of Android.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.020
It would be sad if you'd gone
and paid $100, $200, even more

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.640
for your phone, but
then feel the need

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:55.590
to pay $10 every
month to get security.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:09:01.420
But what we have here with
SafetyNet offers that built in.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.410
So SafetyNet broadly
maps into four segments,

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.750
and this roughly forms
the agenda for this talk.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:08.830
And then we're going to look
at some of these in detail.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.280
First, Verify Apps, and this
is what most people's minds

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:15.790
jumped to when they think about
Google's security services

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:16.960
for Android.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.250
This is in the realm of harmful
app detection, prevention,

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:21.850
and removal.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.900
I'll show you some new features
in Verify Apps and even

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:32.260
some APIs, too,
that you could use.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.670
Then we have the sensor network,
which is rather more elusive.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:39.390
and this is our
privacy-preserving security,

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.000
analytics, and telemetry
framework built

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.350
into every Android device.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:49.080
This is how we can see
across the Android ecosystem,

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.820
the health of Android devices.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.860
It allows us to monitor
the behavior of apps

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.162
and see when things
step out of line.

00:09:58.162 --> 00:10:00.120
And when something unusual
happens on a device,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.630
we can use this sensor network
to root cause that change,

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:08.500
and then go and remediate
where we are able.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.170
The sensor network observes
things like critical security

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:14.217
settings, device
integrity, and hundreds

00:10:14.217 --> 00:10:15.800
of other signals,
which we'll look at,

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.840
and I'll show you some examples
of how that's interpreted.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.082
Next is SafetyNet
developer APIs,

00:10:23.082 --> 00:10:25.060
and this is an area
we've been investing

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.420
in a lot in the last year.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.960
We want to provide
ways for all developers

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.811
to interrogate the state
of security devices.

00:10:34.811 --> 00:10:36.310
It's all well and
good for us to say

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:41.380
Android is secure, but
actually for an app or any code

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.740
running on the device to be
able to make good decisions

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:47.530
about that is important.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:50.120
And this is getting more and
more important every year.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.270
As we see, many of
the critical decisions

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:56.900
related to security being
taken not just at the operating

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:59.750
system level, but at
the application level,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:03.110
so I'll cover three different
APIs in this session, which

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.350
I think could be of value.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.240
And last, we have
Android Device Manager.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.790
If I was to rank these four
in order of importance,

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:14.680
this would clearly
be at the top.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.480
This is addressing
the largest risk

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.870
Android users face when
it comes to security,

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:23.740
and that risk is, of
course, losing a phone.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:25.540
While we spend a
lot of time, and we

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:27.950
will in this talk,
thinking about harmful apps

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.640
or those sort of
attacks, the reality

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.620
is that the average
Android user will never

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:33.940
encounter one of these.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.040
But nearly every Android
user will at some point

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.040
misplace, lose, or have
their phone stolen.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.490
So we need to have a
tool to help users out

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:43.470
in that occasion.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:47.380
So Android Device Manager is
our tool to offer peace of mind

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.062
in their time of need.

00:11:49.062 --> 00:11:50.770
And I'll show you a
little bit about that

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:51.936
towards the end of the talk.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.200
So the good news about
all of these features

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:03.570
is that they are available
across most active Android

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:04.070
devices.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.509
There's no need to have a new
version of Android to use them.

00:12:07.509 --> 00:12:09.050
They're built on
top of the platform.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:11.549
They're deployed through Google
Play Services and the Google

00:12:11.549 --> 00:12:12.810
Play Store.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:15.476
And they are
constantly updating.

00:12:15.476 --> 00:12:16.850
So I hope this
comes as good news

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.070
that users don't need to be
buying a new phone to get

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:19.910
these protections.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:21.869
In fact, a lot of the
work we do is making sure

00:12:21.869 --> 00:12:24.285
that users who are running
older versions of the operating

00:12:24.285 --> 00:12:26.660
system, potentially ones that
aren't receiving security

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:31.430
updates, will still get
the core protections.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.380
Here's the scale at
which we operate.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:44.012
We're protecting over 1.4
billion devices all the time.

00:12:44.012 --> 00:12:45.470
The unique thing
about SafetyNet is

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.770
that we have an unparalleled
view into the Android ecosystem

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:52.640
to spot when things go wrong
and to offer protection.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.120
We spot anomalies, and we can
see trends that are emerging.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.220
We scan devices
pretty rigorously.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:03.480
We're currently at about 750
million device scans a day.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:05.864
If I was to have given this
talk a couple of months ago,

00:13:05.864 --> 00:13:07.530
that number would
have been 400 million.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.870
We're ramping up
very aggressively.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.190
And I'll show you how
that works in a bit.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.670
But let's begin by
talking about apps.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:21.730
We're scanning over six
billion of them daily.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:25.180
The Google Play Store is
a cornerstone of security

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:27.850
for the Android ecosystem.

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:31.270
Google Play allows users to get
the most out of their devices

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:36.040
by getting apps that they want
to use to enable communication

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.284
or games and so forth.

00:13:37.284 --> 00:13:39.700
And it's also enabling new
business models for developers,

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:42.640
giving them access
to a vast audience

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.140
of potential consumers.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.720
With over a million apps on
Google Play and 65 billion

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.710
downloads, ensuring the safe
distribution of these apps

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:53.971
is imperative.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:00.100
So this diagram describes
at a very high level

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:04.850
the security lifecycle overlap
that is distributed via Play.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.140
So we begin in the
top left, where

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:11.659
apps arrive on the Play Store.

00:14:11.659 --> 00:14:13.200
This is where a
developer will upload

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.960
an app in the form
of an APK file

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:20.439
to the Google Play
Developer Console.

00:14:20.439 --> 00:14:22.230
To have been able to
do this, the developer

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.630
will have had to sign up
for a publisher account.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:26.970
They'll have accepted the
Play developer distribution

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:30.960
agreement, and they will have
to pay a registration fee.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.540
and for that registration
to have been verified,

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.039
details from the Google
account that was used

00:14:36.039 --> 00:14:37.830
and billing information
will have been used

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:43.139
to process that registration.

00:14:43.139 --> 00:14:45.180
So once the app's uploaded,
before it is actually

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.550
published, it passes through
our comprehensive review

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.920
and an analysis pipeline.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:53.130
The first step of this
is manual policy review.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.770
And every app that's
published to Google Play

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:01.400
is manually reviewed by a
human for policy inspection.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.400
So at this point,
we'll look to see,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:06.050
does the app adhere to
Google Play policies?

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:08.180
And we'll check
it doesn't contain

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:11.840
violating content,
things like spam

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:17.389
or illegal content
or impersonation.

00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:18.930
So once it's passed
through, and it's

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.830
got past that part
of the check, we then

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:25.770
enter our security analysis part
of the pipeline, which is where

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.560
automated analysis kicks in.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.650
This is in the realm of things
like inspecting the code that's

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.800
inside the app, the
file is contained,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.839
and also running the app
[INAUDIBLE] emulators.

00:15:36.839 --> 00:15:38.880
I'll give you an in-depth
look at what that looks

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.210
like in a couple of slides.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.205
So this analysis is
performed on all the apps,

00:15:44.205 --> 00:15:46.242
and it passes to
our score engine.

00:15:46.242 --> 00:15:47.700
And the score engine
is really what

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:52.070
makes a decision about whether
the app is good or bad.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:54.300
Either the score engine
notices some behavior,

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.540
which is in line with known
bad behavior, in which case

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:01.352
the app will be rejected,
or sometimes it's

00:16:01.352 --> 00:16:02.310
not sure about the app.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.018
It sees something
suspicious, and then we

00:16:04.018 --> 00:16:06.700
pass it for another
round of manual review.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.160
And at this point, we end up
in manual security review.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.360
This is where it's passed
to an expert in app security

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:13.820
analysis.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.780
And they will go
and review that app,

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.330
and then make a determination,
either rejecting or approving

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:19.350
it.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.860
And the decisions that are
taken by those human analysts

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.460
feed back into
the score engine's

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:27.112
future decision-making.

00:16:27.112 --> 00:16:29.070
So once it's got through
this part of the flow,

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:33.642
we end up all being well, as
is the case for 99% of apps

00:16:33.642 --> 00:16:34.600
in the published state.

00:16:37.299 --> 00:16:39.840
Sometimes there are cases where
we've spotted vulnerabilities

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:41.970
in the app that aren't
intentionally harmful.

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:45.720
Perhaps the developer has
included a vulnerable version

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:46.731
of a library.

00:16:46.731 --> 00:16:48.480
An example of that
would be if you include

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.090
a version of open SSL
that's old and it's

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.900
vulnerable to the Logjam attack.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.650
So we would actually
in this pipeline also

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:57.690
expose to the developer
in the Developer Console

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:59.890
the information about
that app and say, hey,

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:02.010
you should probably
go and fix this issue.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:04.985
There are numerous
such vulnerabilities

00:17:04.985 --> 00:17:06.359
that we will alert
developers to.

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:11.839
So this process is
paralyzed, meaning

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:17.447
that most apps going from
upload to the published state

00:17:17.447 --> 00:17:19.280
get there in just a
couple of hours or less.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:23.470
But it doesn't end there.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.000
Every app is
constantly resubmitted

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.920
through this pipeline,
being reanalyzed

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.790
and rescored every day.

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:32.770
And the score engine
is augmented with data

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.790
from the sensor network.

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:37.630
This is what I referred
to in the previous slide,

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.030
where the sensor network
in Android devices

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:43.162
is reporting back how
those apps are behaving.

00:17:43.162 --> 00:17:44.620
So you can think
about it, where we

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:46.180
have the dynamic
sort of analysis

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:49.090
happening in
Google's Cloud, where

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:50.521
we're emulating these apps.

00:17:50.521 --> 00:17:53.020
We actually have a lot of data
coming back from real devices

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:53.686
running as apps.

00:17:57.777 --> 00:17:59.360
We also have Verify
Apps, so if an app

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:03.580
is determined to be
harmful, Verify Apps

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:06.407
operating on devices makes sure
that no instances of that app

00:18:06.407 --> 00:18:06.990
are installed.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.800
It'll warn users about the
app if it's installed already.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.620
It'll prompt them to uninstall.

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.460
And Verify Apps prevents
the app from being

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:17.130
installed if it's distributed
outside of Play as well.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:19.100
And this is where it
gets really exciting,

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:22.010
because this process
for Google Play

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:26.200
is actually applied
mostly, as I've

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.865
shown here, for apps that we
find outside of Google Play

00:18:28.865 --> 00:18:29.890
too.

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:31.900
In fact, over 90% of
apps that are installed

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:34.270
from unknown sources
or preloaded app

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:38.320
stores or any other source have
passed through our security

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.310
analysis pipelines.

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:45.912
So I've alluded to harmful apps.

00:18:45.912 --> 00:18:48.370
I thought it would be worth us
thinking about what actually

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:50.810
is a harmful app.

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.230
At Google, we use the term,
Potentially Harmful Apps,

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:57.050
to talk about apps that we
consider harmful in Android.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:59.320
And a potentially
harmful app is an app

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.230
that poses a potential
security risk to users,

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:04.850
their device, or their data.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:08.320
And we see these broken down
into two main categories--

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:11.337
those that abuse APIs
and those that exploit

00:19:11.337 --> 00:19:12.670
vulnerabilities in the platform.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:20.940
With API abuse, we see
apps that use Android APIs.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:23.680
They operate within the
Android security model

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:28.470
to perform a behavior that
could be considered harmful.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:30.882
An example of this
would be a phishing app.

00:19:30.882 --> 00:19:33.750
Most phishing apps aren't
exploiting the platform.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:37.050
They're using standard
Android APIs and UI widgets

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.000
to draw a convincing-looking
login screen

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:43.130
to phish for credentials.

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:46.245
And this category of app
in the realm of API abuse

00:19:46.245 --> 00:19:47.430
is by far the most common.

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:52.380
Over 90% of PHAs that we
see are in the realm of API

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:55.330
abuse and social engineering.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:58.200
The other category would
be described as exploits.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.580
This is where you're taking
advantage of a gap or a hole

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:04.660
that has been
unpatched in Android.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:06.520
An example of this
would be rooting,

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:10.120
where people root their device,
or privilege escalation,

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:12.460
where you might break outside
of the application sandbox

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:14.480
in some other way.

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:17.590
And this is less common,
as I say-- fewer than 10%.

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:20.260
And if you're running an Android
device that has got the latest

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.840
security patches, typically,
these sorts of PHAs

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:26.332
are ineffective.

00:20:26.332 --> 00:20:28.290
Nearly every PHA we see
is trying to make money

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:30.420
in some way, and so
it's frankly much

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:33.600
simpler to do that
as a malware offer

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.532
by going down the
API abuse path.

00:20:36.532 --> 00:20:40.344
And the unique thing
about our security team

00:20:40.344 --> 00:20:41.760
is that, of course,
we're not just

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:44.301
looking at what's going wrong
and then trying to go and catch

00:20:44.301 --> 00:20:46.410
those apps, but we're
able to effect change

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:47.850
in the Android platform.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.460
So when we see API abuse, we
can go and improve the APIs

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:53.020
in the future
versions of Android.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:56.950
An example of that would be
we'd seen quite a lot of malware

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:00.280
that abuses the
device admin APIs

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:02.590
to try and take
the device hostage,

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:05.140
prevent themselves
from being uninstalled.

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:08.230
And so in Android N, there were
some improvements to those APIs

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:10.000
to remove that
sort of behavior it

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:11.580
that was being commonly abused.

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:18.230
So with that in mind, I'll
now dive into more detail

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:21.090
here about this app analysis.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:26.230
This is really looking at the
core of our app risk analyzer.

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:28.800
I'll talk about just a
few of the specifics,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:31.910
so we begin with
static analysis.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:35.230
This is where we go and
essentially decompile the app,

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:37.330
look at the static
code, and we match

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.170
that against the known patterns
that are considered harmful.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.780
We extract features
and just look

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:46.770
at everything that's
contained within the APK file.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:50.560
The next part, dynamic analysis,
is a lot more exciting.

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:53.440
This is where we run the
applications in our emulators.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:55.990
Every app we get hold
of runs in our emulators

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:58.984
in the cloud in a real
Android environment.

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:01.150
This is how we detect
behavior that couldn't be seen

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:04.110
through static analysis alone.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.314
We monitor things like
network connections

00:22:06.314 --> 00:22:07.730
or interaction
with other services

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:11.720
on the device to come to a
determination about behavior

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:15.980
that could be
considered harmful.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:17.910
I mentioned third
party reports here.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:19.820
We would never
claim that we were

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:21.800
catching 100% of all bad apps.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:23.652
We're always trying
to improve it.

00:22:23.652 --> 00:22:25.610
And one of the things
that the Android security

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:26.984
team has built
out is very active

00:22:26.984 --> 00:22:29.102
relationships with the
research community,

00:22:29.102 --> 00:22:31.310
as well as being provided
with information that helps

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:34.220
us improve this detection.

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:36.770
So we have academic
institutions,

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:38.860
security companies,
independent researchers,

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:41.180
who are all tipping
us off about new types

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:42.230
of bad apps they find.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.020
We build the protections
into this analyzer service.

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.520
And so fostering
those relationships

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.980
is very important.

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:52.730
Developer relations,
developer relationships--

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:54.590
this is where, of
course, when we see apps,

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.900
either through Play or
even outside of Play,

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:59.705
we try and map the link
between those developers.

00:22:59.705 --> 00:23:01.830
Through Play, it's, of
course, a little bit easier,

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:03.890
because we have Google
accounts, and we'll try and find

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:06.350
the relationships between
those accounts, other activity

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:08.340
they've performed on
Google properties,

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:10.310
and we find the
link between apps.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.660
Even outside of Play,
features of the app,

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:15.530
such as the signing certificate
or even the structure

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:17.960
of the app itself give
us a clue as to the link

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:23.050
between developers and
helps us incriminate apps

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:27.755
based on other apps
from the same developer.

00:23:27.755 --> 00:23:29.130
Next, we have the
sensor network,

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:30.540
and this is what I mentioned.

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:33.700
This is the feedback loop
for devices on the field.

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:36.510
This really is the unique
thing about our analysis.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.047
We're really able to augment
what we see with the behavior

00:23:39.047 --> 00:23:39.630
apps for real.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:44.960
This feedback comes
into the risk analyzer

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.210
and helps us identify
apps that maybe

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:52.580
weren't exhibiting that harmful
behavior in the emulators.

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:56.040
And similarity analysis--
of course, most harmful apps

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:57.930
we see are not brand new.

00:23:57.930 --> 00:24:00.420
They're not unique strategies
that have been taken,

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:02.640
and so we're able to compare
the features of the code

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.181
to see if they look like other
ones we've previously flagged.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:11.800
And we're not doing
this on a whim.

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:15.200
We have 20,000 dedicated
CPUs running 24 hours a day,

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:19.150
seven days a week
operating this pipeline.

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:22.030
Each day, we're analyzing
over half a million apps.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.130
As I mentioned, we get apps
not just from Google Play,

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:29.710
and in the last 30
days, we've crawled apps

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:33.420
from over 100,000
different websites.

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:36.360
And increasingly, we're able to
turn to new forms of analysis.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.530
Machine learning models
operating in our app risk

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.610
analyzer have caught
over 50% of harmful apps

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:44.700
that were submitted to Play
in the last month alone.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:46.860
I'll show you an example
of that later on.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:54.920
So with that in mind,
what's the end result?

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:56.590
This would be
useless if we didn't

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:59.026
think it was having an effect.

00:24:59.026 --> 00:25:01.819
At most conferences in
the technology world,

00:25:01.819 --> 00:25:04.110
you're led to expect that
all charts should be going up

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:05.340
and to the right.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.410
So I'm sorry to disappoint
you that this one is

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:10.620
looking rather flat, but
that's for good reason.

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:14.480
Contrary to many of the
reports in the media,

00:25:14.480 --> 00:25:18.590
mobile malware is not the next
major threat to civilization.

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:20.780
So this chart shows
the percentage

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:24.230
of Android devices,
which have a known

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:26.120
potentially harmful
app installed

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.130
over the last couple of years.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:31.275
And you'll see at the end
of 2016, we're hovering

00:25:31.275 --> 00:25:35.052
and, in fact, declining a
little bit around 0.69%.

00:25:35.052 --> 00:25:36.727
So that's less
than 1% of devices

00:25:36.727 --> 00:25:38.810
that we've detected have
a potentially harmful app

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.400
installed.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.842
And that's the overall PHA
rate for the ecosystem.

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:46.300
We can break it down in many
ways that give us a little bit

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:49.450
more nuance into that figure.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:53.260
For example, there's huge
geographic variation here.

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:55.660
One of the big focus
areas for our team

00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:59.200
has been looking at
PHA campaigns operating

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:02.432
in the Southeast Asia
region, especially

00:26:02.432 --> 00:26:04.390
on very low end phones,
where, perhaps, they're

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:06.430
not certified Android devices.

00:26:06.430 --> 00:26:08.620
Some of them are even coming
with malware preloaded

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:10.740
on the device out of the store.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:14.600
And so that's something we're
working really hard to tackle.

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:19.190
In fact, of the uncertified,
unapproved Android devices,

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:22.670
they have a 500% higher chance
of seeing a potentially harmful

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:23.791
app installed.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:30.370
So if we split into
those users, who

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:33.470
are installing from Google
Play and those who are not

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:38.070
and using other sources, we
see another stark contrast.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:40.790
The Google Play figure
is the line at the bottom

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:45.580
here, where we see that
just 0.05% of devices

00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:49.264
have encountered a
potentially harmful app.

00:26:49.264 --> 00:26:51.930
And if you're operating inside a
managed environment, of course,

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.460
you can inform where your users
end up on this sort of chart.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:58.320
You can decide to
restrict your users

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:00.240
to install from the Play Store.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:01.980
You can even go a
step further if you're

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:06.450
in a managed profile by
using the managed Play

00:27:06.450 --> 00:27:09.010
features to restrict the
apps that are available.

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:12.270
So I hope this is reassuring in
that the instances of malware

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:16.650
that we see are
really quite rare.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:18.730
In the next-- in
a few weeks time,

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:22.780
we're going to be releasing our
2016 annual Android security

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:24.114
year-end review.

00:27:24.114 --> 00:27:25.530
so I'd encourage
you to come check

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:27.279
that out, because
actually, it's many more

00:27:27.279 --> 00:27:31.520
insights like these and lots
of other data from last year.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:40.020
So based on having
identified harmful apps,

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:41.887
what actually
happens on devices?

00:27:41.887 --> 00:27:43.470
This screen on the
right is what you'd

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:45.900
see if you were
trying to install

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.420
a known PHA from
an unknown source

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.650
or from a third party store.

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:53.727
As a reminder, of course,
if we detect any PHA,

00:27:53.727 --> 00:27:55.310
that app will no
longer be distributed

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:56.143
through Google Play.

00:27:56.143 --> 00:27:58.370
So at install time,
a user would never

00:27:58.370 --> 00:28:00.740
need to be worried about
installing a harmful app

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:01.640
from the Play Store.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:06.420
So we verify every
installation that

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:09.291
happens outside of Google Play.

00:28:09.291 --> 00:28:11.770
That includes any OEM
store that's pre-loaded,

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:15.490
any carrier store, or
unknown sources that

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:17.640
installs from third
party app stores

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:20.830
that the user might have
downloaded or from file

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:24.010
sharing, peer to
peer, or the web.

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:29.610
And the result of this is that
Verify Apps will either warn,

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.880
block, or approve the install.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:36.210
This dialog is showing what
happens when we warn to user.

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.727
There are some types
of harmful apps

00:28:37.727 --> 00:28:40.060
that we allow the user to
proceed with the installation.

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:42.130
In fact, if you were to
click More Details here,

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:44.671
you'd see there is a button that
allows you to go and install

00:28:44.671 --> 00:28:45.590
the app.

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:47.260
And sometimes we
get asked, well,

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:49.900
why do we allow users
to install harmful apps?

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:52.420
And the answer to that is that
not every potentially harmful

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:55.541
app is not wanted by the user.

00:28:55.541 --> 00:28:57.040
The most clear
example of that would

00:28:57.040 --> 00:29:01.390
be rooting apps, where there
are some number of Android users

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:04.390
who would like to try and root
their device to try and get

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:06.420
new capabilities.

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:09.410
And so our warning dialogs
are mindful of that.

00:29:09.410 --> 00:29:14.050
We're not restricting that sort
of behavior in the ecosystem.

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:17.740
We perform a lot of US
experiments with these dialogs.

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:20.920
In fact, we
reshuffled the screen

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:25.000
that you see here at the end
of 2015 to hide that Install

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.620
Anyway button under
the More Details,

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:29.260
and we instantly
saw a 50% reduction

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:31.780
in the number of harmful
apps being installed.

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:34.221
So we are trying very
hard to make sure

00:29:34.221 --> 00:29:35.470
that users do the right thing.

00:29:37.934 --> 00:29:39.350
And we're not just
warning on apps

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:40.640
that we know to be harmful.

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:42.181
Sometimes we'll have
other clues that

00:29:42.181 --> 00:29:46.465
help us identify whether this
install might be of concern.

00:29:46.465 --> 00:29:47.840
An example of that
would be if we

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:52.760
know it's been installed by
an unusual UID, a Linux UID--

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.400
it's not coming from
an app or process

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:59.369
that we expect to have the
permission to install an app.

00:29:59.369 --> 00:30:01.160
Or maybe we see the
install being triggered

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:02.180
by another harmful app.

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:04.640
We would warn the
user in that case.

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:08.124
And to give you
a sense of scale,

00:30:08.124 --> 00:30:10.540
you might be wondering, how
often do we show these things?

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:13.220
So I looked up the numbers.

00:30:13.220 --> 00:30:17.580
For the length of time that I'm
talking today, for yesterday,

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:21.770
at the same time of day, we
showed 1,000 of these warnings

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:23.310
to people in the US.

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:25.820
So it is not
disproportionately large,

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.480
but I hope that gives you an
idea of how frequently we're

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:32.110
having to warn
about harmful apps.

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:36.910
On the flip side of Verify
Apps is the periodic scans.

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:39.430
All installed apps,
regardless of origin,

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:42.520
are routinely checked for PHAs.

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:45.350
When we find a PHA, we
put it into the stop state

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:48.070
and make sure the app
isn't running anymore.

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:51.574
And then we either warn the
user or remove the app entirely.

00:30:51.574 --> 00:30:52.990
And this notification
on the right

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:55.000
is what happens when we
warn a user about a PHA

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.130
that's been found.

00:30:57.130 --> 00:30:59.310
There's an Uninstall
button there,

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:03.400
or they can tap for the
notification to find out more.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:05.800
And uninstalling
PHAs is made easy

00:31:05.800 --> 00:31:07.750
by the application sandbox.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:10.930
The fact that apps are contained
within an application sandbox

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:13.974
means that uninstalling apps
is not a very complicated task.

00:31:13.974 --> 00:31:16.140
We're able to very cleanly
remove them from devices.

00:31:19.122 --> 00:31:20.580
For the last year
and a half, we've

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:24.860
been scanning every device
in Russia daily for PHAs.

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:27.060
That was to tackle some
very persistent SMS

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:31.140
forward campaigns, and we saw
a huge improvements in the PHA

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:34.550
rates for Russia in doing so.

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:36.990
And so we're excited at
the start of this year

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:39.780
to now be expanding that
coverage to every Android

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:40.950
device in the world.

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.470
So every device will be
checking for installed PHAs

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:46.800
daily in just a few weeks time.

00:31:51.686 --> 00:31:53.310
And so Verify Apps
isn't just operating

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:57.120
there behind the scenes without
you knowing as an application

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:58.256
developer.

00:31:58.256 --> 00:32:00.130
So I'm excited to show
you some new APIs that

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:04.230
are coming in the forthcoming
release of Google Play Services

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:07.065
that you could inspect for
the state of Verify Apps

00:32:07.065 --> 00:32:09.600
and take control.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:14.070
So we have an API called
isVerifyAppsEnabled.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.230
It essentially does
what it says on the tin.

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:17.915
Verify Apps is
enabled by default

00:32:17.915 --> 00:32:20.040
for anyone who signs
into an Android device

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:23.340
with a Google account or
uses the Google Play Store.

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:26.600
And for anyone who doesn't fall
into either of those groups,

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:29.340
but who side loads apps,
installs from unknown sources,

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:32.230
they'll be prompted to enable
Verify Apps at each time

00:32:32.230 --> 00:32:34.020
that they install.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:37.010
So you can check if
Verify Apps is enabled.

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.390
If not, you can call the
enableVerifyApps API.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:42.475
That will prompt the user
to enable Verify Apps.

00:32:42.475 --> 00:32:44.182
And we have the
listHarmfulApps API.

00:32:44.182 --> 00:32:46.140
That will tell you about
any known harmful apps

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:49.150
a user has willingly
installed on that device.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.030
I'll give you a code
example in a second.

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:54.880
I'll mention, finally, that
today already, the platform

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:57.862
has a restriction that you
can put in place if you are

00:32:57.862 --> 00:32:59.320
able to use the
DevicePolicyManager

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:02.800
APIs for a managed device to
ensure that Verify Apps is

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:03.840
enabled.

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:05.732
That forces Verify
Apps to be turned on.

00:33:05.732 --> 00:33:07.690
The user couldn't choose
to go and turn it off.

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:13.960
So this is the
listHarmfulApps API.

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:16.220
It's incredibly
straightforward to use,

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.550
and it removes the need for
you to necessarily build

00:33:18.550 --> 00:33:22.180
your own hacky solutions based
on blacklisting, package names,

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:25.030
or other sort of app
matching or sending

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:26.295
that back to your server.

00:33:26.295 --> 00:33:28.430
A use case here
you could consider

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:30.130
is if your enterprise
app, you might

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:33.520
want to be auditing for any
harmful apps that are installed

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:34.270
on users' devices.

00:33:34.270 --> 00:33:37.210
So you could call this API
and send the results back

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:38.990
to your server.

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:42.270
As I mentioned, this API is
part of Google Play Services,

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.780
and you will use it by
connecting to the SafetyNet API

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:48.920
client through the
Google API classes.

00:33:48.920 --> 00:33:51.440
So it returns the
time of the last scan

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:55.370
with the last scan
time function.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:58.160
That'll always be within
the last 24 hours.

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:00.695
We ensure that when you're
calling this API that that

00:34:00.695 --> 00:34:04.660
is a fresh scan available.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.252
If for some reason, Verify Apps
was not enabled, of course,

00:34:07.252 --> 00:34:08.960
at this stage, you
could then go and call

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:12.850
the enableVerifyApps API to
make sure it was turned on.

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:16.020
And then we have
getHarmfulAppsList.

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:18.570
This is a very
underwhelming API call.

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:21.341
99.5% of the time, it will
return absolutely nothing

00:34:21.341 --> 00:34:21.840
at all.

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:23.969
It'll be an empty list,
but let's take a look

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:28.510
at what happens if there
was to be something there.

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.080
So there's a list of
harmful apps data objects,

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.980
and it contains
information about an app

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.835
to identify the harmful one.

00:34:36.835 --> 00:34:38.960
You can send this back to
your server, for example.

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.969
So first, we have a category.

00:34:40.969 --> 00:34:44.420
And this is used to define
what type of harmful app it is.

00:34:44.420 --> 00:34:48.739
An example of this could be
phishing or Trojan or SMS fraud

00:34:48.739 --> 00:34:51.370
or rooting.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:53.802
We published our
classifications of PHAs online.

00:34:53.802 --> 00:34:55.260
You can search for
our white paper,

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:59.250
and it's also documented
in the API documentation.

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:00.910
And then to identify
the specific app,

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:03.100
we include the package
name of the app,

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:06.967
as well as the Sha256#
of the APK file.

00:35:06.967 --> 00:35:08.800
And then we include an
example at the bottom

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:10.420
here of what that
could look like.

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:16.736
Here we have a phishing app and
its package name and digest.

00:35:16.736 --> 00:35:19.850
The digest is a way of uniquely
identifying that APK file.

00:35:23.800 --> 00:35:26.452
So with that covered, let's
talk about the sensor network.

00:35:26.452 --> 00:35:28.410
I think this is one of
the more exciting parts,

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:29.830
because it operates
so behind the scenes,

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:31.371
people don't typically
know about it.

00:35:31.371 --> 00:35:34.247
So I'm glad to share some
of that with you today.

00:35:34.247 --> 00:35:35.830
On the right, you
see a warning dialog

00:35:35.830 --> 00:35:37.615
would pop up when a user--

00:35:37.615 --> 00:35:40.510
when an app tries to send
a message to a premium rate

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:43.540
SMS number.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:45.800
We ask the user, do you
really want to send this?

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:48.250
And the responses to
that dialog are fed back

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:51.370
into the sensor network, which
help us to identify apps that

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:52.655
could be performing SMS fraud.

00:35:52.655 --> 00:35:54.280
This is one example
of the sort of data

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:55.906
that the sensor
network picks up,

00:35:55.906 --> 00:35:57.280
allowing us to
identify bad apps.

00:36:00.337 --> 00:36:01.920
So the sensor network
is fundamentally

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.200
this feedback loop
that pipes back

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:07.540
into our app risk
analyzer, helping

00:36:07.540 --> 00:36:09.860
us to identify new threats.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:13.139
In a sense, SafetyNet is
looking and keeping an eye

00:36:13.139 --> 00:36:14.930
on what happens on our
device after the app

00:36:14.930 --> 00:36:17.570
is installed to make sure
it doesn't step out of line.

00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:20.630
And the changes in
state on that device

00:36:20.630 --> 00:36:24.740
can be attributed back to the
app that was just installed.

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:26.030
There are hundreds of signals.

00:36:26.030 --> 00:36:27.238
Some of them are listed here.

00:36:27.238 --> 00:36:29.540
For example, exploit attempt
logging, system integrity

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:32.237
checks, SELinux
enforcement, network probes,

00:36:32.237 --> 00:36:34.070
which [INAUDIBLE] man
in the middle attacks,

00:36:34.070 --> 00:36:37.070
that sort of thing
that, SMS denials,

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:41.030
and the core system
security settings.

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:44.000
So let's see one example.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:46.700
We perform statistical analysis
on top of this, and, of course,

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:49.460
operating at such huge
scale across over a billion

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:51.950
Android devices allows
us to spot anomalies

00:36:51.950 --> 00:36:54.690
when they crop up.

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:57.350
So this is one
scorer that features

00:36:57.350 --> 00:37:02.470
in the scorer engine called
the dead or insecure score.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:06.110
And what this tracks is how
often devices disappear off

00:37:06.110 --> 00:37:08.900
our radar once an
app is installed.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:11.260
Typically, we expect that
when you install an app,

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:13.100
that device should
carry on checking back

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:15.100
in with the sensor
network the next day

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:17.710
and the next day
and the next day.

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:19.650
And so we measure
for each app what

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:22.200
the probability of that device
checking back in the next day

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:25.391
is, and so we look and compare
that with the ecosystem

00:37:25.391 --> 00:37:25.890
average.

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:27.681
Of course, there is
some drop-off every day

00:37:27.681 --> 00:37:28.950
from normal device usage.

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:30.910
Maybe I'm going to go
and buy a new phone.

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:36.540
But for apps where that differs
significantly from the norm,

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:39.240
we can go and
investigate that app

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:42.210
or very clearly identify
that the app was

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:44.070
doing something anomalous.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:45.870
And so this is what
we use to go and flag

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.844
new apps that cause
devices to go dark,

00:37:48.844 --> 00:37:50.760
maybe because they try
and turn off SafetyNet,

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:52.260
or maybe they
break the device so

00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:56.088
fundamentally that the user
has to go and factory reset it.

00:37:56.088 --> 00:38:01.200
This has been very effective at
detecting new rooting campaigns

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:04.554
that we've looked at, such
as Hummingbad or Ghost Push.

00:38:04.554 --> 00:38:06.220
And we published a
blog post about this,

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:07.230
so if you want to go
and search for it,

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:09.670
you can just search for
dead or insecure score.

00:38:12.915 --> 00:38:14.290
Of course, we're
not just limited

00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:17.680
to conventional
statistical analysis.

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:20.290
Machine learning is playing
an increasingly key role

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:24.420
in how we find harmful apps.

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:26.970
What you see here is
a clustering of apps,

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:31.170
of harmful apps based on their
install patterns generated

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:34.520
with a neural network
using TensorFlow.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:37.270
This uses the technique
called word embeddings, which

00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:40.860
has pioneered in the field of
natural language processing,

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:43.240
but applying it to apps instead.

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:46.780
In a sense, the order in
which apps are installed

00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:49.270
is quite similar to the
order in which words

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:52.049
are constructed in sentences.

00:38:52.049 --> 00:38:53.590
And so we can use
the ordering an app

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:57.294
installs to discern something
about the apps themselves.

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:03.990
So the result of
this neural network

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:07.070
is a vector for every app.

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:10.140
And app vectors are positioned
in vector space, such that apps

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:14.040
that share common contacts or
attributes or patterns show up

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:17.824
in a similar area
or vector space.

00:39:17.824 --> 00:39:20.240
So for example, we'd expect
to find all the messaging apps

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:23.570
showing up in one part of vector
space or all of the gaming apps

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:25.640
in another cluster.

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:28.490
And this is true for PHAs too.

00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:30.960
So in this 2D embedding,
you see here we've

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:33.810
colored in all of the
potentially harmful apps

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:36.270
and their different categories.

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:40.650
And you see that they cluster
in sometimes quite tight groups.

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:43.920
For example, in the top left,
we have a light blue section.

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:46.794
Those are all SMS fraud apps.

00:39:46.794 --> 00:39:48.330
So hopefully, you
can see how we're

00:39:48.330 --> 00:39:50.100
able to use this very
powerfully to detect

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:52.830
new types of harmful
behavior for a new app

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:54.920
that we haven't yet
been able to classify.

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:58.920
If we see that it's embedding,
and vector space shows up

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.920
within that cluster, we
can begin to highly suspect

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:07.260
that app is also being part
of an SMS fraud campaign.

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:10.410
Approaches like these are
greatly improving our ability

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:11.630
to detect new types of harm.

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:18.240
So let's take a look
at the attestation API.

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:23.340
This is one of the
most frequent APIs

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.682
that I discussed with
enterprises and developers.

00:40:25.682 --> 00:40:27.390
I think it could be
powerful to use maybe

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:30.070
for some of your use cases too.

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.186
This screen here
shows Android Pay

00:40:32.186 --> 00:40:34.560
deciding that it hasn't been
able to determine the device

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:38.400
security sufficiently
to be able to run.

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:42.430
They use the attestation
API to determine this.

00:40:42.430 --> 00:40:44.410
Many apps have
questions, such as,

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:47.020
is the app talking to my server?

00:40:47.020 --> 00:40:48.460
Is it actually my app?

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:50.685
Can I trust the platform APIs?

00:40:50.685 --> 00:40:53.510
Is it a genuine device?

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:56.820
Is my app running
on a rooted phone?

00:40:56.820 --> 00:40:58.850
It is the SafetyNet
attestation API

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:02.250
that allows you to assess the
security and compatibility

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.810
of the Android environments in
which your apps are running.

00:41:08.630 --> 00:41:11.460
So I'll briefly talk you
through how this works.

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:14.040
At a high level, what happens
is you'll call the attestation

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:19.250
API on a device, and then
we all run through SafetyNet

00:41:19.250 --> 00:41:22.430
a series of checks to come to
a decision about the device

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:23.750
state.

00:41:23.750 --> 00:41:26.180
The result will come
back from a Google server

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:29.060
as a signed statement
as to the device

00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:31.070
integrity and compatibility.

00:41:31.070 --> 00:41:34.640
And then you can inspect
that signed statement

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:36.410
on a device you trust--

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:38.304
that's normally your server--

00:41:38.304 --> 00:41:40.220
to decide whether or not
you're going to trust

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:43.350
that app or that device.

00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:48.040
So you need both client and
server side implementations.

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:50.670
So you begin by calling the
attest method of SafetyNet API,

00:41:50.670 --> 00:41:55.330
also in Google Play Services,
with a single use token that

00:41:55.330 --> 00:41:56.620
identifies that request.

00:41:56.620 --> 00:42:00.257
That token will have
been sent by your server.

00:42:00.257 --> 00:42:01.840
And then that's the
end of what you're

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:03.190
going to do on your device.

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:05.750
When you get the result back,
you send that to your server.

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:07.600
As you're using the
attestation to decide

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:09.090
if you trust the
device, you don't

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:11.131
want to be doing any checks
on the device itself.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:19.740
So now on the server side,
you pass back this result.

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:22.590
It's in the form of
adjacent web signature.

00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:25.470
And first, you want
to validate the JWS.

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:29.240
So the first step is validating
the SSL certificate chain

00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:32.760
and checking that the leaf
certificate matches what was

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:36.540
issued to you at android.com.

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:39.150
And then you'll verify
that that certificate

00:42:39.150 --> 00:42:42.635
was used to sign the JWS.

00:42:42.635 --> 00:42:44.760
Now you can look at the
actual contents of the JWS,

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:46.260
which I'm showing
on the right here.

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:47.820
So you'll check
the nonce matches

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:49.740
the one that you requested.

00:42:49.740 --> 00:42:52.020
You'll check the
timestamp is acceptably

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:55.710
close to your request time.

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:58.617
And then you can check the
details about the app itself,

00:42:58.617 --> 00:43:00.700
so you can check, does the
package name of the app

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:02.580
match my own app?

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:07.080
Does the signing certificate
hash match my own app?

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:09.450
Even does the digest
of the APK file

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:12.369
itself match the
APK that I produced?

00:43:12.369 --> 00:43:14.160
And this helps you
determine on your server

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:21.550
whether the Android app that is
talking to you is really yours.

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:23.800
Sometimes we won't include
the APK information,

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:26.216
and that's because we can't
determine with any reliability

00:43:26.216 --> 00:43:27.150
that that is your app.

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:31.060
Maybe it's a random
script that is not even

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:33.870
running on Android at all.

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:35.750
And then you can look
at the last two fields.

00:43:35.750 --> 00:43:37.830
We have CTS profile match
and basic integrity,

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:39.580
and those are really
the key results here.

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:41.670
CTS profile match
is telling you,

00:43:41.670 --> 00:43:44.320
is this device
definitely a device

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:46.810
that has been approved
and certified?

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:50.380
All devices that are
published or produced

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:52.480
with Google services
on them have

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:54.550
to go through some
rigorous certification.

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:55.789
So you can check.

00:43:55.789 --> 00:43:56.830
Is it a certified device?

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.990
And you can restrict the
operation of your app

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:00.829
to those devices.

00:44:00.829 --> 00:44:02.620
And then we have the
basic integrity field,

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:05.880
which is a slightly weaker
check than CTS profile match.

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:08.890
That's now ignoring whether or
not the device was certified

00:44:08.890 --> 00:44:11.620
and just checking, does the
basic integrity of this device

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:13.390
look like it is a
compatible real Android

00:44:13.390 --> 00:44:16.430
environment that's not being
tampered with in any way?

00:44:20.550 --> 00:44:21.221
So we got--

00:44:21.221 --> 00:44:23.720
I'll help you out with this,
if you want to go check it out.

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:25.710
I encourage you to
go and see if this

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:28.060
answers some of your
questions that you might have.

00:44:31.050 --> 00:44:33.380
Like many apps are using
their own basic rooting

00:44:33.380 --> 00:44:35.150
checks or integrity
checks on device,

00:44:35.150 --> 00:44:36.980
which are trivially bypassed.

00:44:36.980 --> 00:44:41.687
And so SafetyNet attestation
is the solution for that.

00:44:41.687 --> 00:44:43.270
We're obviously
paying close attention

00:44:43.270 --> 00:44:45.130
to how well was this
working, looking

00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:49.060
at researchers who are trying
to bypass such restrictions.

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:52.054
And as far as we can tell,
it's holding up pretty well.

00:44:52.054 --> 00:44:53.470
In fact, one of
the challenges you

00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:55.930
may face if you're operating
a service that crosses

00:44:55.930 --> 00:44:59.260
many different platforms
is that you're always

00:44:59.260 --> 00:45:01.660
limited by the
weakest client that is

00:45:01.660 --> 00:45:03.820
going to talk to your server.

00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:07.120
So we start to see
maybe Android apps that

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:11.620
start to impersonate iOS
apps or Windows apps or web

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:15.310
clients to try and communicate
with your server end points.

00:45:21.430 --> 00:45:24.160
Another API that we
expose is Safe Browsing.

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:26.500
Safe Browsing is
a Google service

00:45:26.500 --> 00:45:28.420
that's operated for
many years but lets

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:30.130
applications check
whether a given

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:34.920
URL is potentially harmful.

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:37.770
With it, you can check
pages against Safe Browsing

00:45:37.770 --> 00:45:39.870
different threat blacklists.

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:42.460
You could warn users before
they click on a link,

00:45:42.460 --> 00:45:45.510
or you could prevent users
from sharing unsafe links.

00:45:45.510 --> 00:45:47.830
And on the right, you
see the Chrome browser,

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:50.890
which integrates with the
SafetyNet Safe Browsing API

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:53.712
for every URL that
is loaded in Chrome.

00:45:53.712 --> 00:45:55.170
And here, they're
warning the user,

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:58.450
because it looks like this
page distributes malware.

00:46:01.584 --> 00:46:03.500
The Safe Browsing API
is very straightforward.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:06.160
It's pretty much the
code I've shown here.

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:08.860
You first have to register
an API key, which you can get

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:11.170
on the Google Cloud console.

00:46:11.170 --> 00:46:15.160
And then you call look up URI
with the URL and the threat

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:17.920
types that you are
concerned about.

00:46:17.920 --> 00:46:20.260
At the moment, we have
social engineering

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:22.321
and potentially harmful apps.

00:46:22.321 --> 00:46:23.820
And then we give
you a result, which

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:26.580
is whether or not that URL
matched any of the known threat

00:46:26.580 --> 00:46:27.521
types.

00:46:27.521 --> 00:46:29.270
This is a case where
most of the time, you

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:32.480
will also return an empty list,
in which case that's good news.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:34.590
And the Safe Browsing
API is designed

00:46:34.590 --> 00:46:37.160
to be privacy-preserving
and bandwidth-efficient.

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:40.050
The URL that you're checking
with the Safe Browsing API

00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:42.155
is never sent off the device.

00:46:42.155 --> 00:46:43.530
And so this is a
really great way

00:46:43.530 --> 00:46:48.690
of maintaining the
privacy of that link.

00:46:48.690 --> 00:46:51.090
We're also frequently
syncing the blacklist

00:46:51.090 --> 00:46:52.980
on device in a
very efficient way,

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:55.839
so it's not consuming resources
or bandwidth unnecessarily

00:46:55.839 --> 00:46:56.380
for the user.

00:47:00.400 --> 00:47:02.959
So last, we're going to jump
to Android Device Manager.

00:47:02.959 --> 00:47:04.500
If you haven't used
it, I'd encourage

00:47:04.500 --> 00:47:05.300
you to go and check it out.

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:06.758
Android Device
Manager is this tool

00:47:06.758 --> 00:47:12.825
that allows you to find, locate,
ring, wipe any Android device.

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:21.350
And it's built into
every Android device

00:47:21.350 --> 00:47:25.031
running Google
services by default.

00:47:25.031 --> 00:47:26.030
There's no setup needed.

00:47:26.030 --> 00:47:28.155
All you need to do is sign
into the Google account,

00:47:28.155 --> 00:47:29.410
and that then it's enabled.

00:47:29.410 --> 00:47:30.170
It's active.

00:47:30.170 --> 00:47:33.950
You don't even have to have an
app installed on your device.

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:37.880
You're able to go and
load it up off the website

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:40.940
or install the Android
app to go and ring,

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:45.980
lock, or erase any devices that
your account is signed into.

00:47:45.980 --> 00:47:48.130
And we have integrations
with Google search,

00:47:48.130 --> 00:47:50.463
so if you were signed into
your Google account or Google

00:47:50.463 --> 00:47:53.170
search, and you go and
search Find My Phone,

00:47:53.170 --> 00:47:57.170
we'll show you a one-box result
showing where your phone is.

00:47:57.170 --> 00:47:59.512
Or maybe you have an
Android Wear watch,

00:47:59.512 --> 00:48:01.970
and you've misplaced your phone,
but you can use your watch

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:03.760
to ring your phone too.

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:05.870
That's all part of
Android Device Manager.

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:08.120
And this works from
Android 2.3 up,

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:10.490
so it's enabled for pretty
much every device that

00:48:10.490 --> 00:48:11.990
is active today.

00:48:11.990 --> 00:48:13.490
We're locating
hundreds of thousands

00:48:13.490 --> 00:48:15.620
of phones every single day.

00:48:19.730 --> 00:48:21.490
So with that, that's
brought us to the end

00:48:21.490 --> 00:48:26.230
of my survey of all the
components of SafetyNet.

00:48:26.230 --> 00:48:27.940
We talked about
Verify Apps, which

00:48:27.940 --> 00:48:30.610
is harmful app detection
and removal, the sensor

00:48:30.610 --> 00:48:33.590
network, which is
providing security analysis

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:37.240
and telemetry, helping us
to identify harmful apps,

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:39.340
powerful developer
APIs allowing you

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:43.720
to inspect the security of
a device and make decisions,

00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:47.960
and Android Device Manager,
lost device protection.

00:48:47.960 --> 00:48:51.530
And the exciting
thing about this

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:53.960
is that it's updating
all the time.

00:48:53.960 --> 00:48:56.390
Whenever we come and
present what's going on,

00:48:56.390 --> 00:48:58.250
there's always
something new to add.

00:48:58.250 --> 00:49:01.827
We're working every
day to develop

00:49:01.827 --> 00:49:03.410
these and deploy new
versions to offer

00:49:03.410 --> 00:49:05.825
better protection to
users without them

00:49:05.825 --> 00:49:06.950
having to pay for anything.

00:49:09.760 --> 00:49:13.710
So before I take any
questions, just a reminder

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:18.240
that if you're interested
in knowing more about how

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.240
either Android or
Chrome or Google Cloud

00:49:21.240 --> 00:49:23.760
fits into your
enterprise environment,

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:28.350
you can go and visit this not
particularly easy to pass link

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:33.390
to learn more about our
connected workspace information

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:35.580
and sign up to get
advanced access,

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:37.630
perhaps, to some of
our upcoming reports,

00:49:37.630 --> 00:49:40.701
such as the Android
security year review.

00:49:40.701 --> 00:49:42.979
With that, I'd like to
thank you for attending,

00:49:42.979 --> 00:49:44.520
and I'll be around
for any questions.

00:49:44.520 --> 00:49:45.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:49.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

